1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,280
Finally, we need to actually install Flutter. Visit flutter.io, click on Getting Started, and choose the operating system you're on. I'm on Linux, but all the steps are pretty much the same. Follow the directions, and run the command line. When you're done, go ahead and open up the command line, and type flutter doctor.

2
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:29,340
Flutter doctor is an awesome feature that lets you know exactly what's going on with your Flutter installation. As you can see, I'm pretty much good to go.

3
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:35,700
I've got Flutter, I've got the Android toolchain, I've got Android Studio, and I've got IntelliJ Community Edition installed.

4
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:44,320
But I have no connected devices. Don't worry about that. That'll be available once you start an Android virtual device, which you can do right in the IDE.

